WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:02.802 line:85% position:50% size:90% align:middle
Indexed Universal Life is permanent life

2
00:00:02.802 --> 00:00:05.622 line:85% position:50% size:90% align:middle
insurance first — a death benefit for the

3
00:00:05.622 --> 00:00:08.718 line:85% position:50% size:90% align:middle
people you care about, plus a cash value that

4
00:00:08.718 --> 00:00:10.575 line:85% position:50% size:90% align:middle
can grow inside the policy.

5
00:00:11.025 --> 00:00:13.653 line:85% position:50% size:90% align:middle
When you pay a premium, it splits. Part

6
00:00:13.653 --> 00:00:16.415 line:85% position:50% size:90% align:middle
covers the cost of insurance and charges.

7
00:00:16.415 --> 00:00:18.975 line:85% position:50% size:90% align:middle
What is left can go toward cash value.

8
00:00:19.425 --> 00:00:22.335 line:85% position:50% size:90% align:middle
That cash value may receive indexed crediting

9
00:00:22.335 --> 00:00:24.728 line:85% position:50% size:90% align:middle
— tied to how an index moves, but not

10
00:00:24.728 --> 00:00:27.185 line:85% position:50% size:90% align:middle
invested in the index itself. Policies

11
00:00:27.185 --> 00:00:29.901 line:85% position:50% size:90% align:middle
typically use a floor so credited interest

12
00:00:29.901 --> 00:00:32.811 line:85% position:50% size:90% align:middle
does not go below zero for that period, and a

13
00:00:32.811 --> 00:00:35.462 line:85% position:50% size:90% align:middle
cap or participation rate that limits the

14
00:00:35.462 --> 00:00:38.114 line:85% position:50% size:90% align:middle
upside. Policy charges still come out, so

15
00:00:38.114 --> 00:00:40.959 line:85% position:50% size:90% align:middle
cash value can still decline in a flat year.

16
00:00:40.959 --> 00:00:43.675 line:85% position:50% size:90% align:middle
Any numbers you see here are hypothetical.

17
00:00:44.125 --> 00:00:46.869 line:85% position:50% size:90% align:middle
Here is what people often get wrong. An

18
00:00:46.869 --> 00:00:49.964 line:85% position:50% size:90% align:middle
illustration is not a guarantee. If a policy

19
00:00:49.964 --> 00:00:53.130 line:85% position:50% size:90% align:middle
is underfunded, it can lapse. Insurance costs

20
00:00:53.130 --> 00:00:56.084 line:85% position:50% size:90% align:middle
generally rise with age. And loans against

21
00:00:56.084 --> 00:00:59.250 line:85% position:50% size:90% align:middle
cash value have real mechanics and tradeoffs.

22
00:00:59.700 --> 00:01:02.548 line:85% position:50% size:90% align:middle
Who it can fit: someone who wants permanent

23
00:01:02.548 --> 00:01:05.528 line:85% position:50% size:90% align:middle
coverage, can fund it consistently, and wants

24
00:01:05.528 --> 00:01:08.044 line:85% position:50% size:90% align:middle
a clear explanation — not hype. Who it

25
00:01:08.044 --> 00:01:10.428 line:85% position:50% size:90% align:middle
usually does not fit: anyone chasing

26
00:01:10.428 --> 00:01:13.144 line:85% position:50% size:90% align:middle
guaranteed market-beating returns, or who

27
00:01:13.144 --> 00:01:15.925 line:85% position:50% size:90% align:middle
needs short-term, temporary coverage only.

28
00:01:16.375 --> 00:01:20.602 line:85% position:50% size:90% align:middle
Let's figure out whether it actually fits —

29
00:01:20.602 --> 00:01:23.747 line:85% position:50% size:90% align:middle
no pressure, no obligation. Call

30
